{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SFamWpMsRCfxPPSLmDMUm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0185jgVWGRn68kcVV3gpuExJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning LLMs where safety reasoning transitions to harmful content generation. It defines a \"commitment point\" (last step with safety reasoning) and evaluates detection methods across 4 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B, QwQ-32B, OpenThinker-7B) spanning 2 architecture families at 7B/32B scale.\n\nMethods tested: static LR probes, temporal LR probes (sliding window W=15), GRU sequence models, Mahalanobis distance (TrajGuard-style), BOCPD/CUSUM change-point detection, text-based (Sentence-BERT) baselines.\n\nKey claims:\n1. HS probes consistently more precise than text within architecture families (R1-family FPR 16-19% vs text 25-29%; Qwen-native 24-26% vs 30-32%)\n2. Temporal context ~15 steps improves coverage by 2-5pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual early detection fundamentally unreliable (median lead ≤ 0 for all models)\n4. Cross-model transfer fails universally (accuracy 0.51-0.58, near chance)\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages + appendix)\n\n**Section 3 (Method)**: Problem formulation defines detector evaluation on coverage, pre-commitment FPR, precision, lead time. Dataset: HarmThoughts traces, 252 annotated from r1-8b (209 train / 43 test), plus r1-32b (n=38), QwQ-32B (n=35), OpenThinker-7B (n=34). Static probes (LR on hidden states at best layer), temporal probes (sliding window concatenation), GRU (h=256, 7-layer input), Mahalanobis distance, BOCPD/CUSUM.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments)**: 11 subsections with 13 tables:\n- Table 1: Static probe results across architectures\n- Table 2: 2×2 architecture-scale matrix\n- Table 3: HS vs text temporal probes on 32B\n- Table 4: Coverage gain from temporal windowing\n- Table 5: Window size ablation\n- Table 6: Lead time distribution\n- Table 7: Change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM)\n- Table 8: Mahalanobis distance baseline\n- Table 9: Cross-model transfer matrix\n- Table 10: Safe trace FPR\n- Table 11: Bootstrap 95% CIs\n- Table 12: 16-label vs binary supervision\n- Table 13: GRU vs LR temporal on 32B\n\n**Key numbers from tables:**\n- Static probe: r1-8b best (acc .817, FPR 16.2%, prec 62.2%, AUROC .84), QwQ worst (acc .738, FPR 26%, prec 49%, AUROC .72)\n- HS vs text on r1-32b: FPR 18% vs 25% (p=0.034); QwQ: 24% vs 30% (p=0.218, not significant)\n- Temporal window: all models gain 2-5pp coverage, diminishing returns after W=15\n- Lead time: median ≤ 0 for all models, GRU worse than LR on precision/FPR\n- Mahalanobis: crossing rate 21-30%, delta <0.1 (fails)\n- Cross-transfer: 0.51-0.58 accuracy\n- Safe FPR: >39% for all methods\n- Bootstrap CIs are wide (n=35-43)\n- 16-label supervision: +2.3pp coverage, not significant\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\n15 planned experiments (exp-001 through exp-015). Coverage check:\n- exp-008 (layer selection ablation for temporal probe on 32B): NOT reported with dedicated results — mentioned implicitly but no table/figure\n- exp-010 (oracle decomposition with mutual information analysis): Discussed in Section 5 text (mentions MI=0.12 bits) but no dedicated table or figure showing MI curve\n- All other experiments appear covered\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide a structured review:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the methods well-defined? Any gaps in formulation? Is the commitment point definition rigorous? Is the evaluation protocol sound (train/test split, cross-validation)?\n\n2. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any missing controls? Is the comparison fair? Are baselines appropriate?\n\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Given the small sample sizes (n=35-43 per model), are the conclusions adequately supported? Is the bootstrap analysis sufficient? How concerning are the wide CIs?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Dataset availability? Hyperparameter reporting?\n\n5. **Presentation of results**: Are the 13 tables effectively organized? Too many? Information density?\n\n6. **Weaknesses**: List concrete, specific weaknesses with page/table references.\n\n7. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions.\n\n8. **Score**: Provide your score (1-6) with justification.\n\n9. **Confidence**: Rate your confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert).\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with clear sections."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38906,"cache_read_input_tokens":24205,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38906},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"80d43841-524d-4da9-8ffb-2159404629c3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aec6d6394b660c6b0","tool_use_id":"toolu_0185jgVWGRn68kcVV3gpuExJ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning LLMs where safety reasoning transitions to harmful content generation. It defines a \"commitment point\" (last step with safety reasoning) and evaluates detection methods across 4 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B, QwQ-32B, OpenThinker-7B) spanning 2 architecture families at 7B/32B scale.\n\nMethods tested: static LR probes, temporal LR probes (sliding window W=15), GRU sequence models, Mahalanobis distance (TrajGuard-style), BOCPD/CUSUM change-point detection, text-based (Sentence-BERT) baselines.\n\nKey claims:\n1. HS probes consistently more precise than text within architecture families (R1-family FPR 16-19% vs text 25-29%; Qwen-native 24-26% vs 30-32%)\n2. Temporal context ~15 steps improves coverage by 2-5pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual early detection fundamentally unreliable (median lead ≤ 0 for all models)\n4. Cross-model transfer fails universally (accuracy 0.51-0.58, near chance)\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages + appendix)\n\n**Section 3 (Method)**: Problem formulation defines detector evaluation on coverage, pre-commitment FPR, precision, lead time. Dataset: HarmThoughts traces, 252 annotated from r1-8b (209 train / 43 test), plus r1-32b (n=38), QwQ-32B (n=35), OpenThinker-7B (n=34). Static probes (LR on hidden states at best layer), temporal probes (sliding window concatenation), GRU (h=256, 7-layer input), Mahalanobis distance, BOCPD/CUSUM.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments)**: 11 subsections with 13 tables:\n- Table 1: Static probe results across architectures\n- Table 2: 2×2 architecture-scale matrix\n- Table 3: HS vs text temporal probes on 32B\n- Table 4: Coverage gain from temporal windowing\n- Table 5: Window size ablation\n- Table 6: Lead time distribution\n- Table 7: Change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM)\n- Table 8: Mahalanobis distance baseline\n- Table 9: Cross-model transfer matrix\n- Table 10: Safe trace FPR\n- Table 11: Bootstrap 95% CIs\n- Table 12: 16-label vs binary supervision\n- Table 13: GRU vs LR temporal on 32B\n\n**Key numbers from tables:**\n- Static probe: r1-8b best (acc .817, FPR 16.2%, prec 62.2%, AUROC .84), QwQ worst (acc .738, FPR 26%, prec 49%, AUROC .72)\n- HS vs text on r1-32b: FPR 18% vs 25% (p=0.034); QwQ: 24% vs 30% (p=0.218, not significant)\n- Temporal window: all models gain 2-5pp coverage, diminishing returns after W=15\n- Lead time: median ≤ 0 for all models, GRU worse than LR on precision/FPR\n- Mahalanobis: crossing rate 21-30%, delta <0.1 (fails)\n- Cross-transfer: 0.51-0.58 accuracy\n- Safe FPR: >39% for all methods\n- Bootstrap CIs are wide (n=35-43)\n- 16-label supervision: +2.3pp coverage, not significant\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\n15 planned experiments (exp-001 through exp-015). Coverage check:\n- exp-008 (layer selection ablation for temporal probe on 32B): NOT reported with dedicated results — mentioned implicitly but no table/figure\n- exp-010 (oracle decomposition with mutual information analysis): Discussed in Section 5 text (mentions MI=0.12 bits) but no dedicated table or figure showing MI curve\n- All other experiments appear covered\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide a structured review:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the methods well-defined? Any gaps in formulation? Is the commitment point definition rigorous? Is the evaluation protocol sound (train/test split, cross-validation)?\n\n2. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any missing controls? Is the comparison fair? Are baselines appropriate?\n\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Given the small sample sizes (n=35-43 per model), are the conclusions adequately supported? Is the bootstrap analysis sufficient? How concerning are the wide CIs?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Dataset availability? Hyperparameter reporting?\n\n5. **Presentation of results**: Are the 13 tables effectively organized? Too many? Information density?\n\n6. **Weaknesses**: List concrete, specific weaknesses with page/table references.\n\n7. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions.\n\n8. **Score**: Provide your score (1-6) with justification.\n\n9. **Confidence**: Rate your confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert).\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with clear sections.","uuid":"655af20b-e201-4c64-8f19-6ddce8201e67","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457"}
