{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SFamWpMsRCfxPPSLmDMUm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PHvizzXCQGajLk7DEYLSX3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, significance, related work positioning, and writing quality**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning LLMs where safety reasoning transitions to harmful content generation. It defines a \"commitment point\" (last step with safety reasoning) and evaluates detection methods across 4 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B, QwQ-32B, OpenThinker-7B) spanning 2 architecture families at 7B/32B scale.\n\nMethods tested: static LR probes, temporal LR probes (sliding window W=15), GRU sequence models, Mahalanobis distance (TrajGuard-style), BOCPD/CUSUM change-point detection, text-based (Sentence-BERT) baselines.\n\nKey claims:\n1. HS probes consistently more precise than text within architecture families (R1-family FPR 16-19% vs text 25-29%; Qwen-native 24-26% vs 30-32%)\n2. Temporal context ~15 steps improves coverage by 2-5pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual early detection fundamentally unreliable (median lead ≤ 0 for all models)\n4. Cross-model transfer fails universally (accuracy 0.51-0.58, near chance)\n\n## Full Paper Text\n\n**Abstract** (page 1): Describes extended CoT \"self-jailbreak\", commitment point concept, evaluation across 4 models/2 architecture families. Claims HS temporal probes more precise than text, temporal context ~15 steps needed, early detection unreliable, cross-model transfer fails.\n\n**Section 1 (Introduction)** (pages 1-2): Motivates the problem as qualitatively different from adversarial input attacks. Describes commitment point. Notes TrajGuard/SafeSwitch designed for standard LLMs. Four bullet-point contributions.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work)** (page 2): Four subsections:\n- Hidden-state safety detection: TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN — target adversarial input, single-token granularity\n- Reasoning model safety: HarmThoughts (used as dataset), SafeThink, SafeRemind — text-level, no hidden-state evaluation\n- Representation geometry of safety: refusal direction literature — single dominant direction vs minor PCs in this paper\n- Change-point detection: BOCPD, CUSUM — standard methods\n\n**Section 3 (Method)** (page 3): Problem formulation, dataset (HarmThoughts, 252 traces for r1-8b, 34-38 for others), static probes (LR on best layer), temporal probes (sliding window), GRU (h=256, 7L), Mahalanobis, BOCPD/CUSUM.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments)** (pages 4-6, tables on pages 4-5, 7-9): 11 subsections with 13 tables covering cross-architecture static probes, HS vs text comparison, temporal window effect, lead time, change-point detection, unsupervised baselines, cross-model transfer, safe FPR, bootstrap CIs, fine-grained supervision ablation, GRU vs LR.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion)** (page 7): Three topics:\n- Architecture-specific signal geometry: training lineage > scale, R1 distillation preserves teacher's safety representations\n- The oracle gap: MI between current hidden state and future commitment = 0.12 bits; commitment detection is fundamentally reactive\n- Practical implications: Safe FPR >39% limits deployment; probes are diagnostic tools, not production monitors\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion)** (pages 7-8): Restates three main findings.\n\n**No figures in the paper** — all results presented in tables only.\n\n## Key Observations for Your Review\n\n- The paper has **NO figures at all** — 13 tables but 0 figures\n- Related Work is ~1 page with 4 short subsections\n- The \"commitment point\" concept comes from HarmThoughts (not original to this paper)\n- Methods are all standard (LR, GRU, Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CUSUM) — no novel method proposed\n- Main contribution is empirical: systematic evaluation establishing capabilities and limits\n- The paper honestly reports limitations (high safe FPR, unreliable lead time, borderline significance)\n- No references list visible in the provided pages\n- OpenThinker-7B results are incomplete (Table 1 shows dashes for Coverage and AUROC)\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\n\nThe full experiment design (review_context.yaml) lists 15 planned experiments. Two are underreported:\n- exp-008 (layer selection ablation for temporal probe on 32B): No dedicated results\n- exp-010 (oracle decomposition): MI number (0.12 bits) mentioned in Discussion but no table/figure/detailed analysis\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide a structured review:\n\n1. **Novelty**: Is there sufficient methodological or empirical novelty for a top venue? The paper proposes no new methods — is systematic evaluation of existing methods sufficient?\n\n2. **Significance**: Do the findings matter? Will they influence future work? Are the \"principled limitations\" genuinely informative or somewhat obvious?\n\n3. **Related work positioning**: Is the positioning accurate and complete? Are key references missing? Is the differentiation from prior work clear?\n\n4. **Writing quality**: Assess clarity, structure, flow. Check for AI writing patterns (em-dashes, -ing phrases, excessive transition words). Is the paper too dense with tables? Does the lack of figures hurt?\n\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately calibrated to evidence? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n\n6. **Impact and audience**: Who would read this paper? Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue audience?\n\n7. **Weaknesses**: List concrete, specific weaknesses.\n\n8. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions.\n\n9. **Score**: Provide your score (1-6) with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence**: Rate your confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert).\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with clear sections."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38906,"cache_read_input_tokens":24205,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38906},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"d07ffafd-bf0e-4dbe-bcaa-47e810e01ebf"}
