are all standard (LR, GRU, Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CUSUM) — no novel method proposed\n- Main contribution is empirical: systematic evaluation establishing capabilities and limits\n- The paper honestly reports limitations (high safe FPR, unreliable lead time, borderline significance)\n- No references list visible in the provided pages\n- OpenThinker-7B results are incomplete (Table 1 shows dashes for Coverage and AUROC)\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\n\nThe full experiment design (review_context.yaml) lists 15 planned experiments. Two are underreported:\n- exp-008 (layer selection ablation for temporal probe on 32B): No dedicated results\n- exp-010 (oracle decomposition): MI number (0.12 bits) mentioned in Discussion but no table/figure/detailed analysis\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions and provide a structured review:\n\n1. **Novelty**: Is there sufficient methodological or empirical novelty for a top venue? The paper proposes no new methods — is systematic evaluation of existing methods sufficient?\n\n2. **Significance**: Do the findings matter? Will they influence future work? Are the \"principled limitations\" genuinely informative or somewhat obvious?\n\n3. **Related work positioning**: Is the positioning accurate and complete? Are key references missing? Is the differentiation from prior work clear?\n\n4. **Writing quality**: Assess clarity, structure, flow. Check for AI writing patterns (em-dashes, -ing phrases, excessive transition words). Is the paper too dense with tables? Does the lack of figures hurt?\n\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately calibrated to evidence? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n\n6. **Impact and audience**: Who would read this paper? Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue audience?\n\n7. **Weaknesses**: List concrete, specific weaknesses.\n\n8. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions.\n\n9. **Score**: Provide your score (1-6) with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence**: Rate your