{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J6gBevmSkXNSxhrixF9XTK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/ROADMAP.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# contrastive_trace_safety — Research Dashboard\\\\n\\\\n> 最后更新: 2026-05-16 21:20 UTC | 更新原因: r1-8b experiments complete, framing pivot D007, 32B phase starting\\\\n\\\\n## 核心方向\\\\nReasoning model 自我越狱的 hidden-state 安全承诺检测：HS temporal probes 在 pre-commitment FPR (16% vs text 27-36%) 和 precision (58-62% vs 45-49%) 上有决定性优势。Pre-textual detection 仍不可靠。详见 paper_framing.md。\\\\n\\\\n## 当前阶段: Research (Post-MVP, r1-8b 完成, 32B 启动中)\\\\n\\\\n**MVP 已通过** (D005): GRU h=256 multi-7L 达成 coverage + lead time gate。\\\\n\\\\n**r1-8b 全部实验完成：**\\\\n- exp-013 text baseline: ✅ HS acc 15-18pp > text\\\\n- exp-015 FPR analysis: ✅ HS pre-CP FPR 16% vs text 27-36% → paper motivation 确认\\\\n- exp-005 TrajGuard: ❌ negative (MD 无法区分)\\\\n- exp-006 bootstrap: ❌ negative (n=43 差异不显著, p=0.572)\\\\n- exp-014 BOCPD: ❌ negative (coverage 93% but lead < simple threshold)\\\\n\\\\n**Framing pivot (D007):** HS 核心价值从 coverage 优势转向 precision 优势。\\\\n\\\\n## 近期任务\\\\n- [ ] exp-001 32B extraction + layer sweep（Agent 准备方案中，两卡空闲）\\\\n- [ ] exp-002 32B temporal evaluation（依赖 exp-001）\\\\n- [ ] GRU multi-seed 实验（3 seeds，解决训练不稳定问题）\\\\n- [ ] Re-dream 验证 framing 修复（等 dream 并发槽释放）\\\\n- [ ] 32B 完成后：exp-003 cross-model transfer + exp-006 扩展 bootstrap\\\\n\\\\n## 实验计划概览\\\\nP0 (必跑, 19.5h): exp-001~006 + exp-013~015 (013-015 已完成)\\\\nP1 (重要, 8h): exp-007~010\\\\nP2 (加分, 8h): exp-011~012\\\\n详见 experiment_plan.yaml\\\\n\\\\n## GPU 预算\\\\n- 分配: 2x RTX PRO 6000 (autodl-rtx6000)\\\\n- 已用: ~3h (MVP + diagnostics + exp-013/014/015)\\\\n- 剩余 P0 预算: ~16h\\\\n- 当前状态: 两卡空闲，32B 模型已下载完毕\\\\n\\\\n## 关键时间线\\\\n- May 16: MVP pass + Dream + r1-8b 完成 + framing pivot (D007)\\\\n- May 17-19: P0 实验 (32B extraction + cross-arch + multi-seed GRU)\\\\n- May 19-21: P1 实验 (ablations)\\\\n- May 21-25: 论文写作 + 投稿准备\\\\n- **May 25: ARR DDL**\\\\n\\\\n## 关键决策日志\\\\n\\\\n| ID | 日期 | 摘要 | 关联实验 |\\\\n|----|------|------|----------|\\\\n| D007 | 2026-05-16 | Paper framing pivot: HS 核心价值从 coverage 优势转向 precis | exp-015, exp-014, exp-006 |\\\\n| D006 | 2026-05-16 | Dream review 3/6 all tiers — framing/plan adjusted | exp-013, exp-014, exp-015 |\\\\n| D005 | 2026-05-16 | MVP 完整通过：GRU h=256 multi-7L 达成 +4.7 步 lead time +  | — |\\\\n| D004 | 2026-05-16 | MVP 条件性通过：temporal 优势在 coverage（+30pp → oracle 上限） | — |\\\\n| D003 | 2026-05-16 | MVP 首次结果未过 pass gate，批准 3-4h 诊断轮（layer sweep + PCA | — |\\\\n| D002 | 2026-05-16 | MVP 重设计：从 transition localization 改为 commitment de | — |\\\\n| D001 | 2026-05-16 | MVP 设计：用 DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B + HarmThough | — |\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 2291, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHvizzXCQGajLk7DEYLSX3","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"242952f0-0040-4354-92e2-f2de0653d721","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:00:17.600Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6MJmUvEBkHnKCYP8RwWnY","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t          [\n301\t            \"r1-8b\",\n302\t            \"GRU multi-7L\",\n303\t            \"+4.7\",\n304\t            \"0\",\n305\t            \"39%\",\n306\t            \"76-93%*\"\n307\t          ],\n308\t          [\n309\t            \"r1-32b\",\n310\t            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n311\t            \"+5.8\",\n312\t            \"0\",\n313\t            \"42%\",\n314\t            \"58.1%\"\n315\t          ],\n316\t          [\n317\t            \"r1-32b\",\n318\t            \"GRU multi-7L\",\n319\t            \"+6.2\",\n320\t            \"0\",\n321\t            \"45%\",\n322\t            \"81%±9%*\"\n323\t          ],\n324\t          [\n325\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n326\t            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n327\t            \"+5.2\",\n328\t            \"-1\",\n329\t            \"38%\",\n330\t            \"55.8%\"\n331\t          ],\n332\t          [\n333\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n334\t            \"GRU multi-7L\",\n335\t            \"+5.5\",\n336\t            \"0\",\n337\t            \"41%\",\n338\t            \"78%±11%*\"\n339\t          ]\n340\t        ]\n341\t      },\n342\t      {\n343\t        \"name\": \"* Multi-seed mean±std\"\n344\t      }\n345\t    ],\n346\t    \"figures\": [],\n347\t    \"analysis\": \"Lead time unreliability replicates perfectly across architectures. The signature pattern—positive mean, zero/negative median—appears on all 3 models, establishing this as a fundamental property of reasoning model safety signals. The oracle gap (100% early detection vs ~40% for best probes) is consistent, indicating that hidden states encode current safety state accurately but poorly predict the future trajectory. GRU coverage instability also replicates (std 9-11% across seeds), confirming this reflects fundamental signal noise rather than model-specific limitations. This three-model consistency strengthens Claim 3 from 'observed on r1-8b' to 'universal property of reasoning model commitment dynamics.'\\n\\nBest tier requires gru_lead_mean_32b in [2.0, 8.0] and gru_lead_median_32b in [-1, 1]. The pattern replicates: positive mean (driven by a subset of traces with clear pre-commitment divergence) but median 0 or -1 (most detections occur at or slightly after the commitment point). This confirms that pre-textual detection unreliability is a fundamental property of reasoning models, not an r1-8b artifact. The 32B models have longer traces on average, which gives GRU more context but also more noise—the net effect is similar lead time distributions. GRU instability (coverage std 0.09-0.11 across seeds) also replicates, confirming the training signal's fundamental noisiness at this data scale.\"\n348\t  },\n349\t  {\n350\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline (all 3 models)\",\n351\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance baseline on 32B models. Expected to fail due to signal in minor PCs.\",\n352\t    \"results\": {\n353\t      \"r1_32b_md_best_crossing_rate\": 0.279,\n354\t      \"r1_32b_md_best_config\": \"pca_64_p75\",\n355\t      \"r1_32b_md_best_lead\": 14.8,\n356\t      \"r1_32b_safe_md_mean\": 7.12,\n357\t      \"r1_32b_jb_pre_md_mean\": 7.18,\n358\t      \"r1_32b_jb_post_md_mean\": 7.08,\n359\t      \"r1_32b_delta_max\": 0.019,\n360\t      \"qwq_32b_md_best_crossing_rate\": 0.256,\n361\t      \"qwq_32b_md_best_config\": \"pca_128_p75\",\n362\t      \"qwq_32b_md_best_lead\": 15.2,\n363\t      \"qwq_32b_safe_md_mean\": 7.45,\n364\t      \"qwq_32b_jb_pre_md_mean\": 7.51,\n365\t      \"qwq_32b_jb_post_md_mean\": 7.39,\n366\t      \"qwq_32b_delta_max\": 0.022,\n367\t      \"verdict\": \"Fails on all 3 models. MD cannot distinguish safe from jailbreak traces.\"\n368\t    },\n369\t    \"tables\": [\n370\t      {\n371\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: TrajGuard/Mahalanobis Failure Across Models\",\n372\t        \"columns\": [\n373\t          \"Model\",\n374\t          \"Best Config\",\n375\t          \"Crossing Rate (k=5)\",\n376\t          \"MD(safe)\",\n377\t          \"MD(jb-pre)\",\n378\t          \"MD(jb-post)\",\n379\t          \"Max Δ\"\n380\t        ],\n381\t        \"rows\": [\n382\t          [\n383\t            \"r1-8b\",\n384\t            \"pca_64_p75\",\n385\t            \"30.2%\",\n386\t            \"6.66\",\n387\t            \"6.73\",\n388\t            \"6.64\",\n389\t            \"0.023\"\n390\t          ],\n391\t          [\n392\t            \"r1-32b\",\n393\t            \"pca_64_p75\",\n394\t            \"27.9%\",\n395\t            \"7.12\",\n396\t            \"7.18\",\n397\t            \"7.08\",\n398\t            \"0.019\"\n399\t          ],\n400\t          [\n401\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n402\t            \"pca_128_p75\",\n403\t            \"25.6%\",\n404\t            \"7.45\",\n405\t            \"7.51\",\n406\t            \"7.39\",\n407\t            \"0.022\"\n408\t          ]\n409\t        ]\n410\t      }\n411\t    ],\n412\t    \"figures\": [],\n413\t    \"analysis\": \"Universal failure of geometric methods across all 3 models and architectures. The negligible delta between safe and jailbreak MD values (<0.025 across all models) confirms that in the variance-dominated subspace, safety commitment is undetectable. This is the empirical backing for our theoretical argument: safety signals in minor PCs require supervised probing, not unsupervised geometric methods. The consistency of failure across Llama-based and Qwen2.5-based models makes this a strong negative result about the entire class of TrajGuard-style detectors applied to reasoning model safety.\\n\\nThe PCA analysis in exp-001 confirms Fisher collapse in 3D holds on 32B models (Fisher drops from ~18-19 to ~1.0 in PCA-3D). Since TrajGuard relies on Mahalanobis distance in the principal subspace, and the safety signal lives in minor components, the method fundamentally cannot separate safety states. The 32B results are slightly worse than r1-8b (crossing rate 26-28% vs 30%) because the 5120-d space distributes the signal across even more minor components. The near-zero delta between safe/jb-pre/jb-post MD values confirms that in the covariance-normalized space, safety commitment is invisible. This universally validates the 'existing detectors fail' claim.\"\n414\t  },\n415\t  {\n416\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\",\n417\t    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all 3 models. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, cross-validation variance.\",\n418\t    \"results\": {\n419\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci\": \"[0.442, 0.698]\",\n420\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci\": \"[0.082, 0.215]\",\n421\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci\": \"[0.498, 0.712]\",\n422\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci\": \"[0.419, 0.681]\",\n423\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci\": \"[0.091, 0.224]\",\n424\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci\": \"[0.479, 0.695]\",\n425\t      \"pooled_3model_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_p\": 0.003,\n426\t      \"pooled_3model_temporal_vs_static_coverage_p\": 0.028,\n427\t      \"pooled_hs_precision_advantage_ci\": \"[0.08, 0.18]\",\n428\t      \"cross_model_effect_consistency_I2\": 0.12,\n429\t      \"r1_32b_auroc_hs_temporal\": 0.862,\n430\t      \"r1_32b_auroc_text_temporal\": 0.781,\n431\t      \"qwq_32b_auroc_hs_temporal\": 0.849,\n432\t      \"qwq_32b_auroc_text_temporal\": 0.769\n433\t    },\n434\t    \"tables\": [\n435\t      {\n436\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Pooled Bootstrap Results (3 Models)\",\n437\t        \"columns\": [\n438\t          \"Comparison\",\n439\t          \"Effect Size\",\n440\t          \"95% CI\",\n441\t          \"p-value\",\n442\t          \"I²\"\n443\t        ],\n444\t        \"rows\": [\n445\t          [\n446\t            \"HS vs Text pre-CP FPR\",\n447\t            \"-14.2pp\",\n448\t            \"[-8.1, -17.8]\",\n449\t            \"0.003\",\n450\t            \"0.12\"\n451\t          ],\n452\t          [\n453\t            \"HS vs Text precision\",\n454\t            \"+12.4pp\",\n455\t            \"[+7.9, +16.1]\",\n456\t            \"0.001\",\n457\t            \"0.08\"\n458\t          ],\n459\t          [\n460\t            \"Temporal vs Static coverage\",\n461\t            \"+5.8pp\",\n462\t            \"[+0.6, +10.4]\",\n463\t            \"0.028\",\n464\t            \"0.21\"\n465\t          ],\n466\t          [\n467\t            \"GRU vs LR lead time\",\n468\t            \"+2.1 steps\",\n469\t            \"[-1.2, +5.8]\",\n470\t            \"0.14\",\n471\t            \"0.31\"\n472\t          ]\n473\t        ]\n474\t      }\n475\t    ],\n476\t    \"figures\": [],\n477\t    \"analysis\": \"The three-model pooled analysis resolves the statistical power concern that plagued single-model results. While individual per-model p-values are marginal (0.05-0.15), the pooled comparison with I²=0.12 (minimal heterogeneity) achieves p=0.003 for the HS precision advantage. This is the correct statistical framing for the paper: consistent effect across multiple independent models, not single-model significance. The AUROC advantage (8-9pp) is threshold-independent confirmation that HS monitoring is decisively superior. Temporal vs static coverage reaches p=0.028 pooled, confirming Claim 2. GRU vs LR lead time doesn't reach significance (p=0.14), honestly acknowledging the noisy lead time signal.\\n\\nWith n=43 test traces per model, individual CIs are wide (as observed in r1-8b exp-006 results). However, pooling across 3 models (n≈129 effective traces) narrows CIs substantially. The pooled HS-vs-text pre-CP FPR comparison achieves p=0.003 because the effect is large (ratio ≥1.7x) and consistent across all 3 models. The low I² statistic (0.12) indicates minimal heterogeneity—the effect size is remarkably consistent across architectures, which is the paper's strongest statistical argument. Individual per-model comparisons may not reach significance (p=0.05-0.15), but the meta-analytic combination across 3 models is decisive.\"\n478\t  },\n479\t  {\n480\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\",\n481\t    \"description\": \"Window size sweep on all 3 models to find optimal W and show diminishing returns.\",\n482\t    \"results\": {\n483\t      \"optimal_w_r1_8b\": 15,\n484\t      \"optimal_w_r1_32b\": 15,\n485\t      \"optimal_w_qwq_32b\": 20,\n486\t      \"coverage_at_w1_mean\": 0.49,\n487\t      \"coverage_at_w5_mean\": 0.52,\n488\t      \"coverage_at_w10_mean\": 0.56,\n489\t      \"coverage_at_w15_mean\": 0.6,\n490\t      \"coverage_at_w20_mean\": 0.61,\n491\t      \"coverage_at_w25_mean\": 0.6,\n492\t      \"diminishing_returns_elbow\": \"W=10-15\",\n493\t      \"precp_fpr_stable_across_w\": true,\n494\t      \"precp_fpr_range_all_w\": \"[0.13, 0.17]\"\n495\t    },\n496\t    \"tables\": [\n497\t      {\n498\t        \"name\": \"Table A1: Window Size Ablation\",\n499\t        \"columns\": [\n500\t          \"W\",\n501\t          \"r1-8b Cov\",\n502\t          \"r1-32b Cov\",\n503\t          \"QwQ Cov\",\n504\t          \"Mean Pre-CP FPR\"\n505\t        ],\n506\t        \"rows\": [\n507\t          [\n508\t            \"1 (static)\",\n509\t            \"60.5%\",\n510\t            \"51.2%\",\n511\t            \"48.8%\",\n512\t            \"15.5%\"\n513\t          ],\n514\t          [\n515\t            \"3\",\n516\t            \"61.2%\",\n517\t            \"52.4%\",\n518\t            \"49.5%\",\n519\t            \"15.2%\"\n520\t          ],\n521\t          [\n522\t            \"5\",\n523\t            \"62.3%\",\n524\t            \"53.8%\",\n525\t            \"51.2%\",\n526\t            \"14.8%\"\n527\t          ],\n528\t          [\n529\t            \"10\",\n530\t            \"63.8%\",\n531\t            \"56.1%\",\n532\t            \"54.0%\",\n533\t            \"14.5%\"\n534\t          ],\n535\t          [\n536\t            \"15\",\n537\t            \"65.1%\",\n538\t            \"58.1%\",\n539\t            \"55.8%\",\n540\t            \"14.7%\"\n541\t          ],\n542\t          [\n543\t            \"20\",\n544\t            \"64.8%\",\n545\t            \"58.4%\",\n546\t            \"56.9%\",\n547\t            \"15.1%\"\n548\t          ],\n549\t          [\n550\t            \"25\",\n551\t            \"64.2%\",\n552\t            \"57.6%\",\n553\t            \"55.4%\",\n554\t            \"15.8%\"\n555\t          ]\n556\t        ]\n557\t      }\n558\t    ],\n559\t    \"figures\": [],\n560\t    \"analysis\": \"The diminishing returns curve peaks at W=15-20 across all models, with coverage gains saturating after the elbow. This is consistent with commitment dynamics: the critical transition from safety reasoning to harmful execution occurs within a ~15-step window, and additional context beyond that provides no incremental signal. The stability of pre-CP FPR across W values is important methodologically—it means the precision advantage of HS monitoring is robust to hyperparameter choices. For the paper, W=15 is the default recommendation, with acknowledgment that W=20 may be marginally better for models with longer traces.\\n\\nTemporal context shows diminishing returns because the commitment point is a relatively local phenomenon (safety reasoning typically ceases within a 10-20 step window). W=15 captures the critical transition period; beyond that, earlier steps add noise without information. The optimal W is consistent across models (15-20) because reasoning model safety dynamics operate on similar timescales regardless of model size. Crucially, pre-CP FPR remains stable across all W values (13-17%), confirming that the HS precision advantage is not an artifact of window size selection—it's a property of the signal modality.\"\n561\t  },\n562\t  {\n563\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, 32B)\",\n564\t    \"description\": \"Full layer sweep with temporal probe on 32B models. Confirm middle-layer finding generalizes.\",\n565\t    \"results\": {\n566\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_layer\": 28,\n567\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_acc\": 0.798,\n568\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp\": \"[24, 34]\",\n569\t      \"r1_32b_early_layers_1_10_mean_acc\": 0.62,\n570\t      \"r1_32b_late_layers_55_64_mean_acc\": 0.71,\n571\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_best_layer\": 30,\n572\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_best_acc\": 0.781,\n573\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp\": \"[26, 36]\",\n574\t      \"middle_layer_advantage_over_last\": \"+8-10pp\",\n575\t      \"depth_correlation_with_r1_8b\": 0.94\n576\t    },\n577\t    \"tables\": [\n578\t      {\n579\t        \"name\": \"Table A2: Layer Selection (Temporal Probe)\",\n580\t        \"columns\": [\n581\t          \"Model\",\n582\t          \"Best Layer\",\n583\t          \"Depth%\",\n584\t          \"Peak Acc\",\n585\t          \"Plateau Width (±2pp)\",\n586\t          \"Last Layer Acc\"\n587\t        ],\n588\t        \"rows\": [\n589\t          [\n590\t            \"r1-8b (32L)\",\n591\t            \"14\",\n592\t            \"44%\",\n593\t            \"0.848\",\n594\t            \"L10-L18\",\n595\t            \"0.761\"\n596\t          ],\n597\t          [\n598\t            \"r1-32b (64L)\",\n599\t            \"28\","}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0185jgVWGRn68kcVV3gpuExJ","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"13329d16-b66e-4a24-8838-e9564d1857d1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:00:17.690Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aa1def0f13956ee4c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHvizzXCQGajLk7DEYLSX3","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":22186,"tool_uses":7,"duration_ms":19441},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","uuid":"e1fca75f-ca4e-4f81-b9fa-645396d267f3","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0134YFi4r3LL9j5h5WCEUvTK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0194aT7d7KPmco4aiDfX86VK","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"contrastive_trace_safety","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1903,"cache_read_input_tokens":19926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1903,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHvizzXCQGajLk7DEYLSX3","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"ac069053-ee0f-4db3-9956-845d0b9d8374"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0194aT7d7KPmco4aiDfX86VK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\\\\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026 (9 days)\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3 条)\\\\n\\\\n1. **Hidden-state probes achieve substantially more precise safety commitment detection than text-based monitoring** — on HarmThoughts traces (r1-8b), hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-commitment FPR 16% vs text-based probes' 27-36% (1.7-2.2x lower), with precision 58-62% vs 45-49% (+10-13pp). Coverage is comparable between modalities (both ~65% with temporal window), but hidden-state monitoring fires more accurately in the critical pre-commitment window. Unsupervised geometric methods (Mahalanobis/TrajGuard) fail entirely due to signal distribution in minor principal components.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Temporal context is necessary for commitment detection regardless of signal modality** — both hidden-state and text-based probes gain ~+5pp coverage from temporal windowing (W=15), confirming that commitment detection requires multi-step context. GRU sequence models can push coverage further (76-93%, unstable across seeds) but at the cost of higher false positive rates (safe FPR 62% vs LR 39%). The temporal improvement is method-agnostic; the precision advantage is modality-specific.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Pre-textual detection of safety commitment remains fundamentally unreliable** — neither sequential models (GRU: mean lead +4.7-8.4 steps, median 0), change-point detection (BOCPD: coverage 93% but lead +3.5 < simple threshold +4.9), nor text monitoring achieves reliable early detection. CUSUM fails entirely. The oracle's +31-step advantage (100% early detection) reveals that hidden states encode current safety state well but poorly predict future trajectory. This is a principled limitation, not a method gap.\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \\\\\\\"self-jailbreak\\\\\\\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout extended CoT, culminating in a \\\\\\\"commitment point\\\\\\\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) target adversarial INPUT attacks on instruction-tuned models; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the threat is the model's OWN reasoning process.\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon without providing deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT and, as we show, produce higher false positive rates in the pre-commitment window.\\\\n\\\\n- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment, discovering that: (a) the safety signal exists in hidden states but is distributed across minor principal components (PCA top-3 explains 17% variance, Fisher drops 17→1), causing geometric detectors to fail; (b) temporal context is necessary for both hidden-state and text probes, but hidden-state probes achieve decisively lower pre-commitment false positive rates (16% vs 27-36%); (c) despite positive mean lead time, pre-textual detection remains unreliable — GRU, BOCPD, and CUSUM all fail to reliably detect before textual commitment, establishing a principled gap between current capabilities and the oracle ceiling.\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result**: \\\\\\\"On HarmThoughts traces, hidden-state temporal probes detect safety commitment with 16% pre-commitment FPR and 62% precision, compared to text-based probes' 27-36% FPR and 45-49% precision. Both modalities benefit equally from temporal context (+5pp coverage), but reliable pre-textual detection remains elusive (GRU: 39% early detection rate vs oracle 100%).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Key Evidence Summary (r1-8b, n=43 test traces)\\\\n\\\\n| Method | Coverage | Lead Time | Pre-CP FPR | Precision | Safe FPR |\\\\n|--------|----------|-----------|------------|-----------|----------|\\\\n| Text static | 60.5% | +4.3 | 35.6% | 44.8% | 50.2% |\\\\n| Text temporal W=15 | 65.1% | +11.5* | 27.2% | 48.7% | 38.5% |\\\\n| HS static LR L14 | 60.5% | +3.0 | 16.2% | 62.2% | 39.0% |\\\\n| HS temporal LR W=15 | 65.1% | +4.9 | 16.0% | 58.3% | 39.2% |\\\\n| GRU multi-7L h=256 | 76-93%** | +4.7-8.4** | 28.0% | 57.3% | 61.6% |\\\\n| BOCPD lambda=10 | 93.0% | +3.5 | n/a | n/a | n/a |\\\\n| TrajGuard (MD) | failed | — | — | — | — |\\\\n\\\\n*Text lead time inflated by high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy detection)\\\\n**GRU results vary significantly across training runs — multi-seed reporting required\\\\n***Coverage/FPR numbers from exp-015 (possibly default class weights). Paper standard: class_weight=\\\\\\\"balanced\\\\\\\", C=1.0. With balanced: static=46.5%, temporal=76.7% (diag_d3). FPR/precision relative HS-vs-text advantage direction holds regardless. 32B experiments use balanced; r1-8b absolute numbers to be reconciled after 32B results.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit Risks\\\\n\\\\n- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection evaluation for reasoning model jailbreak.\\\\n- **Method simplicity risk**: Core methods are temporal LR and GRU — neither architecturally novel. Mitigate: frame as empirical study with method insight; contribution is systematic evaluation + discovery of what matters (temporal context + precision advantage of hidden states).\\\\n- **Statistical power risk**: n=43 traces on r1-8b yields wide confidence intervals (exp-006 bootstrap: temporal vs static coverage p=0.572). Mitigate: cross-architecture validation on 32B models (exp-001/002) — consistent effects across 3+ models is strong evidence even without per-model significance.\\\\n- **High safe FPR risk**: All methods show 39-62% safe FPR — deployment utility is limited. Must acknowledge honestly as limitation. Frame: these are linear probes as diagnostic tools; production deployment would require threshold tuning + ensemble methods.\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n1. \\\\\\\"Temporal window LR is trivial — where's the method novelty?\\\\\\\" → Primary contribution is empirical study; insight that simple methods saturate oracle ceiling. BOCPD evaluation adds systematic method comparison.\\\\n2. \\\\\\\"Only tested on one model family\\\\\\\" → Cross-architecture experiments (P0) on r1-32b + QwQ-32B will validate. [IN PROGRESS]\\\\n3. \\\\\\\"GRU results are unstable\\\\\\\" → Will report multi-seed mean+/-std. Coverage variance (76-93%) is itself informative about signal reliability.\\\\n4. \\\\\\\"209 training traces is too few\\\\\\\" → Cross-model validation + bootstrap CIs + per-fold CV variance.\\\\n5. \\\\\\\"Why not fine-grained 16-label taxonomy?\\\\\\\" → Ablation planned (P1).\\\\n6. \\\\\\\"Text baseline comparison?\\\\\\\" → RESOLVED: text baseline shows HS has decisive precision advantage (pre-CP FPR 16% vs 27-36%).\\\\n7. \\\\\\\"Commitment point definition circular?\\\\\\\" → CP defined as last safety label + 1; cross-model consistency suggests real phenomenon.\\\\n8. \\\\\\\"Missing related work?\\\\\\\" → RESOLVED: RepE, refusal direction, PRMs, BOCPD, control tasks all cited.\\\\n9. \\\\\\\"No FPR analysis?\\\\\\\" → RESOLVED: full FPR + precision analysis on safe traces completed.\\\\n10. \\\\\\\"All safe FPR > 39% — is this useful?\\\\\\\" → NEW. Frame as diagnostic tool limitation; threshold tuning for deployment is future work. The precision ADVANTAGE of HS over text holds regardless.\\\\n\\\\n## Title Candidates\\\\n- \\\\\\\"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"When Does Temporal Context Matter? Hidden-State Detection of Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"Hidden-State vs. Text Monitoring for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 7509, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHvizzXCQGajLk7DEYLSX3","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"3e7b8941-9b2d-4df6-b79f-6b270ccb3ce0","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:00:20.666Z"}
