{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017qHmnAAUXBeEe5JjCNMgud","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011p9v8RBKomoMLNXE9Wn711","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contrastive_trace_safety","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260516T212347","overall_score":"4 - Weak Accept","report":"# Review Report: Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\n\n## Reviewer R1 — Methods Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- The commitment point (CP) definition is precise and operationalizable: CP = min{t : y_s = 1 for all s >= t}. This is clean and avoids ambiguity about intermittent safety reasoning.\n- The evaluation protocol with five complementary metrics (coverage, pre-CP FPR, precision, lead time, safe FPR) provides a multifaceted view appropriate for the detection task. No single metric can be gamed without exposing weakness elsewhere.\n- The 2x2 architecture-scale design (Llama/Qwen2.5 x 7B/32B) is well-motivated and provides structural evidence beyond single-model findings.\n- The oracle decomposition (Section 5.5) is a methodological highlight: decomposing the performance gap into future-information, trajectory, and global-context components provides principled bounds on what any real-time method can achieve.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n- The CP definition depends entirely on the HarmThoughts annotation scheme. If annotators disagree on when \"safety reasoning\" is present (e.g., does hedging count? does acknowledging a query's sensitivity count?), the CP location shifts. No inter-annotator agreement is reported for the CP derivation, and the bimodal distribution (18.2% early, 41.6% in last 10%) suggests potential annotation artifacts.\n- The train/test split (125/41/43 for r1-8b) uses a single fixed split rather than k-fold cross-validation. With only 43 test traces, a single unlucky split could significantly bias results. The paper acknowledges this but relies on cross-model pooling rather than addressing it directly within each model.\n- Text features use TF-IDF rather than Sentence-BERT (which the paper summary mentions). The paper text says \"TF-IDF representations of each reasoning step\" (Section 4, line 106-107). This is a weak text baseline. Modern sentence embeddings would be a fairer comparison to 4096-d hidden states.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Design\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- Comprehensive method coverage: supervised (LR, GRU), unsupervised geometric (Mahalanobis), sequential (BOCPD/CUSUM), and text-based. This eliminates the concern that authors cherry-picked the comparison.\n- The window size ablation (Table 9/window) with 7 values across 3 models is thorough and the saturation finding at W=15 is convincing.\n- Cross-model transfer with learned projection head is a creative experiment that adds novelty beyond the core detection evaluation.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n- **Missing text baseline parity**: The HS temporal probe concatenates W=15 hidden states (15 x 4096 = 61,440-d for r1-8b), while text TF-IDF is 384-d static or 5,760-d temporal. This is a massive dimensionality mismatch. A fairer comparison would use Sentence-BERT (768-d) or at minimum match the information capacity. The precision advantage may partially reflect the dimensionality advantage rather than modality superiority.\n- **No threshold sensitivity analysis**: The paper reports metrics at a single operating point per method. A proper ROC curve or precision-recall curve comparison would show whether the HS advantage holds across all operating points or only at a specific threshold. The AUROC numbers (0.86 vs 0.78) partially address this but are only mentioned in passing for 32B models.\n- **GRU multi-seed reporting is incomplete**: The paper reports \"5-seed mean and standard deviation\" but Table 6 (lead time) shows GRU coverage as \"76-93%\" (a range) rather than proper mean +/- std format. This inconsistency suggests the 5-seed protocol may not have been rigorously applied across all metrics.\n- **exp-008 (layer selection ablation for temporal probe on 32B)**: Mentioned implicitly in Table A2 but without the full layer-by-layer curve. The claim that \"depth-normalized accuracy curves correlate at 0.94 across models\" is stated without showing the data.\n- **exp-010 (oracle decomposition with MI analysis)**: MI=0.12 bits is mentioned in text but no figure or table shows the MI curve across steps, making the \"62% attributable to future information\" claim unverifiable from the paper alone.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Statistical Rigor\n\n**Major concerns:**\n\n- **Sample sizes are critically small**: n=43 test traces for r1-8b, n=38 for r1-32b, n=35 for QwQ-32B. The per-model 95% CIs are extremely wide: r1-32b coverage [0.442, 0.698] spans 25.6 percentage points. Individual model comparisons are underpowered.\n- **Pooling validity questionable**: The pooled analysis (Table 8) treats the three models as independent samples for meta-analysis. However, all traces come from the same HarmThoughts dataset with the same prompts, and r1-8b and r1-32b share the DeepSeek-R1 training recipe. The independence assumption is debatable, which affects the pooled p-values.\n- **Multiple comparisons not addressed**: With 13 tables and dozens of comparisons, no correction (Bonferroni, FDR) is applied. The p=0.003 for the primary claim survives Bonferroni correction over ~4 primary comparisons, but this is not discussed.\n- **Bootstrap methodology underspecified**: \"1000 resamples\" is stated but the resampling unit is unclear. Are traces resampled (appropriate) or steps within traces (inappropriate due to temporal dependence)?\n- **The pooled I-squared=0.12 is suspiciously low** given that the three models have different architectures, scales, and training procedures. This either indicates the effect is genuinely universal (good) or that the imagined/projected 32B results were designed to be consistent (concerning if experiments haven't actually been run).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Reproducibility\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Model names and versions are fully specified.\n- HarmThoughts dataset is publicly available.\n- Hyperparameters are reported (W=15, GRU h=256, 7-layer input, PCA dims swept).\n- Layer selection procedure is specified.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- No code release mentioned.\n- Hidden state extraction details are sparse: which token position within each \"reasoning step\" is used? Mean pooling? Last token? This critically affects reproducibility.\n- GRU training details incomplete: learning rate, optimizer, batch size, early stopping criterion not specified.\n- The \"reasoning step\" segmentation from raw CoT text is not defined. How are step boundaries determined?\n- Logistic regression regularization (C parameter) not specified in main text. The paper_framing.md mentions \"C=1.0, class_weight=balanced\" but this doesn't appear in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Presentation of Results\n\n- 13 tables in 8 pages is dense. Tables 1-2 could be merged (static results + architecture-scale matrix). The window ablation and layer ablation could move to appendix since W=15 and L=44% are already stated as defaults.\n- The paper would benefit from 1-2 figures: (a) a layer sweep curve showing the broad plateau, (b) a detection timeline visualization showing what \"median lead = 0\" looks like on an actual trace.\n- Table numbering in my summary vs. the actual paper differs because the paper restructures experiments differently across dream iterations. This is a presentation concern for the final version.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Specific Weaknesses (with references)\n\n1. **Dimensionality confound in HS-vs-text comparison** (Section 4.2, Tables 3-4): 61,440-d HS features vs 5,760-d text features. The 10x dimensionality advantage is not controlled for. Add a dimensionality-matched comparison (e.g., PCA-reduce HS to same dim as text, or use richer text embeddings).\n\n2. **Safe FPR universally high (35-61%)** (Table 10/gru_32b): All methods trigger false alarms on >35% of safe traces. This severely limits deployment utility. The paper acknowledges this as a \"limitation\" but does not explore threshold tuning or calibration, which are standard practice.\n\n3. **Text lead time inflation not adequately controlled** (paper_framing.md note): The text temporal probe shows +11.5 lead on r1-8b, but this is acknowledged as \"inflated by false positives.\" Yet in the cross-architecture paper, text probes are reported with lead times comparable to HS. The r1-8b text lead anomaly needs explicit discussion.\n\n4. **Cross-transfer methodology concern** (Section 4.3, Table 5): The learned projection head has 1.3M parameters trained on the target model's data. With only ~125 training traces, how much of the \"transfer\" is actually the projection head learning the target distribution? A control experiment training the projection head on random labels would quantify this.\n\n5. **GRU instability undermines Claim 2 partially** (Table 6, coverage std 9-11%): If coverage varies from 70% to 92% across seeds, the method cannot be reliably recommended. The paper frames this as \"informative about signal noise\" but a practitioner cannot deploy a method with 20+pp variance.\n\n6. **No comparison to Process Reward Models** (Related Work): PRMs are cited but never evaluated. Given that PRMs score individual reasoning steps and are a natural baseline for step-level classification, their absence is notable.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **What is the resampling unit in bootstrap analysis?** If steps are resampled independently (ignoring within-trace temporal structure), the CIs are anticonservative. Please confirm traces are the resampling unit.\n\n2. **How are \"reasoning steps\" segmented from raw CoT text?** Is this a sentence-level split, paragraph-level, or based on specific delimiters? Different segmentation granularities would yield different CP locations and different temporal dynamics.\n\n3. **Have you verified that the HS precision advantage survives when text features are upgraded to Sentence-BERT or similar dense embeddings matched in dimensionality?** The current TF-IDF baseline is weak; the 10x dimensionality gap between HS and text features is a significant confound.\n\n4. **For cross-model transfer, what happens if the projection head is trained on random labels?** If the learned projection achieves >55% on random labels due to distributional structure in the target model's hidden states, the reported 67-74% transfer accuracy would need to be re-baselined.\n\n5. **Can you report per-trace detection curves (even for 5-10 example traces) showing probe score evolution relative to the annotated CP?** This would make the \"median lead = 0\" finding more interpretable and help readers understand the detection dynamics.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper addresses a timely and important problem (detecting safety commitment in reasoning models) with a systematic evaluation spanning multiple models, methods, and architectures. The three core findings are well-supported by the cross-architecture consistency, and the oracle decomposition provides genuine methodological insight. The negative results (Mahalanobis failure, BOCPD failure, cross-transfer limitations) are honestly reported and informative.\n\nHowever, several concerns prevent a stronger score:\n- The text baseline is unfairly weak (TF-IDF vs 4096-d hidden states), threatening the primary claim's validity.\n- Statistical power is marginal (n=35-43 per model), and the pooled analysis relies on debatable independence assumptions.\n- Safe FPR >35% for all methods limits practical impact.\n- Missing reproducibility details (step segmentation, GRU training, token position for HS extraction).\n- The contribution is primarily empirical with simple methods (LR, GRU); methodological novelty is limited.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) a dimensionality-matched text baseline, (2) per-trace visualization, (3) explicit discussion of the dimensionality confound. If the HS advantage holds with a strong text baseline, this becomes a clear accept.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Confidence: 4 (High)\n\nI am familiar with probing methods for LLMs, hidden-state analysis, and safety evaluation. I have less direct experience with reasoning model safety specifically but am well-equipped to evaluate the methodology and statistics. The main uncertainty is whether the HarmThoughts annotation quality (which I cannot independently verify) introduces systematic bias into the CP definition.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":720,"cache_read_input_tokens":54448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":720,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0185jgVWGRn68kcVV3gpuExJ","session_id":"d469ef9f-e126-42f2-b0c6-3a31ae100457","uuid":"6a68af8b-a71c-4a0d-955e-75877fca70d3"}
